he sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/ca/cae6f56db55b49b98df6ce7e3ae0777e725af14de9dedb5b61b54039bac41860.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $7.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 7$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<7$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $7-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<7.$\n\nTherefore, $[7,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[7,\\infty )=7.\n$$\n\nNotice that $7$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=7\\notin A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489208 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489208 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/64131e649a82e06d2750d53bdf05b1701b0674a05b70149081ddb58d700adb54.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42308119-ffcd-55c6-b052-68e017e7cc90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489208 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,2).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 2$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<2$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<2.$\n\nTherefore, $[2,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[2,\\infty )=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A=(0,2).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8283d19d-ef90-50f2-bc41-5e0573a21b42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489235 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge 2$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[2,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<2$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<2.$\n\nTherefore, $[2,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[2,\\infty )=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2\\notin A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(A)=2\\notin A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A=(-5,5].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "993667c9-c973-5dd1-b35f-eb1848f951dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489247 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -5$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-5]$ contains only lower bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-5$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-5.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-5]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-5]=-5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-5\\notin A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le -5\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(A)=-5\\notin A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489264 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7f8c921-7225-588c-b9e8-a78affb7e519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489264 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/05/05737c6e8e7b88f1a91a5fd6cc7d5b6a088227e327ef66e937e279c090a47256.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $3.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{1}=3+2{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{1}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{1}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{21}{5},\\infty )=\\frac{21}{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{21}{5}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{21}{5}\\in A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489295 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489295 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489295 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489295 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5d/5dc63d364beae0482fdb996372e5042e4c5edf2a6bdbd7a5b79dbcc55cf419cb.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489295 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd9f0c45-62d6-5bab-b897-93c9f4793a8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489295 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-1,4].$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 4$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<4$ cannot be an upper bound of $A$ because $4,$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $[4,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[4,\\infty )=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
